DIAGNOSTIC PATCH (not for production):

The default unix port hangs on real silicon (gf180mcu uLinux SoC, 30 MHz,
16 MiB QSPI PSRAM, no hardware RNG) even for `micropython -c 'pass'`.
The same binary runs fine under QEMU virt. To bisect where it hangs we:

1. Instrument main() / main_() with raw `write(2, ...)` markers before
   and after each major init step (libc entry, stack-limit, malloc,
   gc_init, mp_init, do_str). The markers go to stderr via the raw
   write() syscall so they bypass MicroPython's I/O layer entirely --
   the last marker you see on the serial console identifies the exact
   step that didn't return.

2. Shrink the default heap from 1 MiB to 128 KiB so a slow / failing
   malloc on a 16-MiB-total-RAM noMMU/uClibc target is ruled out.

3. Switch `mp_hal_get_random()` to non-blocking flags (GRND_INSECURE
   then GRND_NONBLOCK) so a never-initialised entropy pool can't be
   the cause. With CONFIG_HW_RANDOM unset the kernel may never seed,
   and libc / mp init paths that call getrandom(.., 0) block forever.

When the bisect is done this patch is dropped.

Signed-off-by: kianv POST diag <noreply@anthropic.com>

